Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillet: of two roundings whose contacts on a face between their edges
cross once (the face narrower than the two roundings from the crossing to
an end), the smaller ball rolls tangent on the larger rounding's blend
from that end to the crossing and runs on the face beyond it, the larger
blend cut by its contact there; roundings of either kind (mitcad#1926,
after 1720).

A wall whose top narrows from 3.5 to 2.5 along its length, its top's edges
rounded with 2 and 1 (either edge slanting), and a step whose height falls
from 2 to 1.3, its foot rounded with 1 (concave) and its top edge with 0.5:
OCCT computed both roundings on the faces of their edges, their contacts on
the face between crossing, and the blends running through each other past
the crossing (a valid-looking result, the wall's with 0.45 mm3 more
material than the shape the two roundings make one after the other).

- ChFi3d_Builder::RideOnBlendPart, from Compute once the stripes are
  computed (before the corners), for each pair of constant roundings of
  one SurfData each, sharing a face F, as RideOnBlend takes them (of the
  other kind, or the later one the smaller of one kind; open contours
  whose ends meet no other stripe), whose contacts on F cross once inside
  both: the ride's end is the end where the rider's contact lies nearer the
  larger one's edge than the larger one's contact; the larger stripe's
  contact there lies on an arc of F, beyond which the face at the end.
  The rider's SurfData is split at the crossing (splitSurfDataAt) and its
  piece beyond the ride kept. The support is the larger blend's surface as
  a face (rideSupport), run on a quarter along its contact and its whole
  width across it, so that the rider's ball, walked between its other face
  and the support over its whole spine as 1720 does, rides on past the
  crossing; its walk's piece through the crossing is split there and its
  piece of the ride kept, its points at the crossing the other piece's.
  Its contact on the support ends where it crosses the face at the end and
  goes onto F with its curve projected there, as 1720's; the larger stripe
  is split at the crossing, its piece of the ride holding that contact
  (reversed, a curve of its own, where the rider runs against it), and F's
  edge at the ride's end, between the two edges, is cut at its vertices.
  ApplyRides then makes the two blends meet on that contact as a part ride
  of 0016 (the larger stripe's piece not its first or last), the points at
  the crossing one.
- StartSolOverflow, for a tangent ride: the contact on F projected onto a
  periodic support is put in the period of the support's bounds (a support
  run on past the blend's contacts holds it one period off).

Rides whose ends meet other stripes (corners of more blends), a face
narrower than the two roundings in its middle only (two crossings) and
pairs whose blends OCCT already finds overlapping on the face (the plate's
back edge beside a tower's foot whose distance from it grows past the two
radii) are not made so.

